    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Step 8: Why Preprocessing is Important\n",
    "\n",
    "The comparison between raw and preprocessed models clearly demonstrates the importance of data preprocessing in machine learning.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Issue in Raw Data | Impact | Fix Applied |\n",
    "|-------------------|--------|-------------|\n",
    "| Class imbalance (~74% vs 26%) | Models biased toward majority class, low recall | SMOTE |\n",
    "| Unscaled features (Total Charges 0-9K vs Tenure 0-72) | Distance-based models (KNN) severely degraded | StandardScaler |\n",
    "| Mixed data types | Models cannot process string columns | LabelEncoder |\n",
    "\n",
    "**KNN showed the most dramatic improvement** from preprocessing - going from the weakest raw model (Recall ~0.45) to the highest-recall preprocessed model (Recall ~0.86). This directly demonstrates the sensitivity of distance-based models to feature scale.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Step 9: Conclusion & Which Model Outperformed\n",
    "\n",
    "**Raw Data:**\n",
    "- Logistic Regression performed best (highest recall among raw models)\n",
    "- KNN was the weakest due to lack of feature scaling\n",
    "\n",
    "**After Preprocessing:**\n",
    "- All models improved dramatically (30+ percentage points in Recall)\n",
    "- **KNN achieved the highest Recall (0.86)** - most churn customers correctly identified\n",
    "- **Logistic Regression achieved the best F1 Score (0.82)** - best precision-recall balance\n",
    "- Decision Tree showed the most overfitting but strong overall recall\n",
    "\n",
    "**Business Recommendation:**  \n",
    "Deploy **KNN** as the real-time churn scoring system (highest recall, fewest missed churners).  \n",
    "Back it up with **Logistic Regression** for interpretability and stability.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Most Important Churn Drivers (from Decision Tree Feature Importance)\n",
    "\n",
    "| Rank | Feature | Business Implication |\n",
    "|------|---------|----------------------|\n",
    "| #1 | Contract Type | Month-to-month customers churn far more than annual/biennial holders |\n",
    "| #2 | Monthly Charges | Higher charges strongly associated with churn risk |\n",
    "| #3 | Total Charges | Lower total charges (shorter tenure) higher churn likelihood |\n",
    "| #4 | Tenure Months | Newer customers significantly more likely to churn |\n",
    "| #5 | Internet Service | Fiber optic subscribers show higher churn rates |"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a3ec2b25",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Market Basket Analysis: Groceries Dataset\n",
    "**Menahil Suleman** &nbsp;&nbsp; \n",
    "**Date:** May 21st, 2026\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## Project Overview\n",
    "\n",
    "This project performs **Market Basket Analysis** on the Groceries transactional dataset\n",
    "using two frequent itemset mining algorithms : **Apriori** and **FP-Growth**.\n",
    "Market Basket Analysis is a data mining technique used by retailers to discover\n",
    "**purchasing patterns**, which products customers tend to buy together.\n",
    "These patterns are expressed as **association rules** of the form:\n",
    "\n",
    "> *\"Customers who buy X also tend to buy Y\"*\n",
    "\n",
    "The goal is not just to find patterns, but to compare how efficiently each algorithm\n",
    "finds them, and to extract **actionable insights** from the rules generated.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## Introduction\n",
    "\n",
    "### What is Market Basket Analysis?\n",
    "Market Basket Analysis (MBA) is one of the most widely used applications of data mining\n",
    "in the retail industry. By analyzing large volumes of transaction data, businesses can:\n",
    "- Identify which products are frequently purchased together\n",
    "- Design better store layouts and product placements\n",
    "- Create targeted promotions and bundle offers\n",
    "- Improve recommendation systems (e.g. \"Customers also bought...\")\n",
    "\n",
    "### Dataset Description\n",
    "The dataset used in this project is the **Groceries Dataset**, a classic benchmark\n",
    "for association rule mining.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Property | Value |\n",
    "|----------|-------|\n",
    "| Total Transactions | 9,835 |\n",
    "| Unique Items | 169 |\n",
    "| Average Basket Size | 4.4 items |\n",
    "| Source | Groceries CSV (R datasets / mlxtend compatible) |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Algorithms Used\n",
    "\n",
    "**Apriori**\n",
    "Apriori is the foundational algorithm for association rule mining. It works by\n",
    "generating candidate itemsets level by level, first finding all frequent single items,\n",
    "then pairs, then triplets, and so on. While easy to understand and implement,\n",
    "it becomes slow on large datasets because it scans the full database once per itemset level.\n",
    "\n",
    "**FP-Growth (Frequent Pattern Growth)**\n",
    "FP-Growth improves on Apriori by compressing the entire transaction database into\n",
    "a compact tree structure called an **FP-Tree**. It then mines frequent patterns\n",
    "directly from the tree without generating candidates, making it significantly\n",
    "faster and more memory-efficient on large datasets.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Metrics Explained\n",
    "\n",
    "| Metric | Formula | What it means |\n",
    "|--------|---------|---------------|\n",
    "| **Support** | P(A ∪ B) | How frequently the itemset appears in all transactions |\n",
    "| **Confidence** | P(B \\| A) | How often the rule is correct when A is purchased |\n",
    "| **Lift** | Confidence / P(B) | How much more likely B is given A, compared to random chance |\n",
    "\n",
    "> A **Lift > 1** means the association is stronger than random.\n",
    "> In this project we filter for **Lift > 1.2** to keep only meaningful rules.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Thresholds Used\n",
    "\n",
    "| Parameter | Value | Meaning |\n",
    "|-----------|-------|---------|\n",
    "| Minimum Support | 0.03 | Item must appear in at least 3% of baskets (~295 transactions) |\n",
    "| Minimum Confidence | 0.40 | Rule must be correct at least 40% of the time |\n",
    "| Minimum Lift | 1.2 | Filters out coincidental co-occurrences |"
   ]
  },
