    "## Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "This assignment implemented and compared two well-known frequent itemset mining algorithms,\n",
    "**Apriori** and **FP-Growth**, on the Groceries dataset (9,835 transactions, 169 unique items).\n",
    "\n",
    "### Key Findings\n",
    "\n",
    "| Metric | Apriori | FP-Growth |\n",
    "|--------|---------|-----------|\n",
    "| Runtime | slower | **faster** |\n",
    "| Frequent Itemsets | same | same |\n",
    "| Rules Generated | same | same |\n",
    "| Avg Lift | same | same |\n",
    "\n",
    "> Both algorithms produce **identical itemsets and rules** at the same thresholds\n",
    "> the difference lies entirely in **efficiency**.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why FP-Growth is Faster\n",
    "- Apriori scans the full database **once per itemset length**, generating large candidate sets.\n",
    "- FP-Growth compresses the entire database into a compact **FP-Tree** in just two passes,\n",
    "  then mines patterns directly from the tree, no candidate generation needed.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Practical Recommendation\n",
    "For small datasets, Apriori is straightforward and easy to trace step-by-step.\n",
    "For large, real-world transactional datasets like this one, **FP-Growth is the preferred choice**\n",
    "due to its significantly lower runtime and memory usage.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Association Rules Insight\n",
    "The top rules consistently showed items like **whole milk**, **other vegetables**, and\n",
    "**rolls/buns** as frequent consequents, confirming these are grocery staples that\n",
    "naturally appear alongside many other items. Lift values above 1.2 confirm these\n",
    "are genuine associations, not coincidental co-occurrences."
   ]
